      "editorial": {
        "marketOverview": "Thanksgiving closes US cash markets. Reuters corroborates Wednesday's recovery and Thursday Asian relief, while oil supply concerns remain elevated. US chip leadership remains unconfirmed.",
        "whatChanged": "Open Watch increased from 178 to 180, adding formal tariff proposals and PCE/GDP upside surprises. Both concern Wednesday developments already reflected in the previous cautious outlook. Reuters syndicated reporting confirms Brent Wednesday settlement at $94.61; curated Treasury pair implies a 21.2 bp curve.",
        "marketInterpretation": "The stronger macro releases reinforce tightening risk, but reported yields declined, consistent with risk aversion rather than a clean hawkish repricing. Tariffs are proposals with hearings in January, not immediate duties. Asian relief is supportive but does not establish US semiconductor recovery.",
        "threeDayPath": "Friday's abbreviated session may extend relief but thin liquidity raises reversal risk. Monday tests the move after Sunday's OPEC+ meeting. Tuesday macro timing and earnings remain unverified; reported JOLTS timing should not be treated as confirmed.",
        "bullCase": "Asian technology recovery, less severe tariff interpretation and broad US participation support stabilization if US chips regain relative strength and oil eases.",
        "bearCase": "Elevated oil, above-consensus core inflation, AI financing and Nvidia rating concerns could renew technology selling. Lower yields alone are not sufficient evidence of improving risk appetite.",
        "watchNext": "Monitor oil and shipping developments, Asian closes, timestamped US futures, Friday early close and Sunday OPEC+. Verify SOX, VXN, Nasdaq breadth, Fed pricing and Tuesday macro/earnings before raising confidence."
